From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: delete render targets the game has drawn over once unused for ten frames (jpkto, zortos293/Lichii #1571), EDEN_UP_STALE_TARGETS=1, off by default

What: Ocarina of Time makes its render targets (the shadow maps among them) anew every frame, at
addresses that shift as the camera moves, so each frame's targets land over older ones; the
texture cache makes a new image for each and flags the overlapped old ones BadOverlap. Our
collector deletes such an image without a copy back, but only once it is 50 frames old while
memory is under the expected mark (25 above it, 10 above critical) and only about ten images a
frame, so they pile up between area loads. With the switch on, TextureCache::RetireStaleTargets
runs every frame in TickFrame, before the area evict and the collector and whatever the memory
use, and deletes a render target when all of this holds:
- BadOverlap; made for a render target binding (new flag RenderTargetOrigin, set by
  FindRenderTargetView on the image JoinImages makes, only while the switch is on); GPU-written,
  not CPU-written; single-sample, backed, not sparse, rescaled, remapped or trimmed; whole 4 KiB
  pages;
- unused (LRU tick) for EDEN_UP_STALE_TARGETS_FRAMES frames (default 10, upstream's constant);
- nothing still needs it: no queued download, asynchronous decode or unswizzle, not a bound
  render target, not decoding; no alias, and no subresource-compatible overlap, used within those
  frames; and (ours, the cold sweep's and the RT evict's rule) the guest never read it back
  (forced_flushed, dma_downloaded, a relocation guard); and (ours, T148 review) it is not one of
  the last four images a present showed: TryFindFramebufferImageView does not touch the LRU, so a
  frame the game holds on screen (a load, a pause) would look unused after n frames and its
  deletion would leave the present with nothing or with the stale guest bytes.
A sampled twin (a BadOverlap alias of it with no other alias, not a render target, not
CPU-written, not costly, converted or decoded) goes with it. At most 512 deletions a frame. The
deletion is the collector's own: MarkStale, untrack, unregister, DeleteImage(id) without
immediate_delete, so the VkImage and its memory wait in gpu_sentenced_images until the GPU has
passed every command recorded with it.

RomainROCH's review point on #1571 (the scan stops after 4,096 examined candidates and always
started at the oldest, so 4,096 kept old targets would hide every target behind them for good):
taken as upstream fixed it in fab2816. A scan that stops at its bound leaves the next one to resume
behind the candidates it examined (the deleted ones are no longer counted); a scan that reaches
the end starts the next round at the oldest. Host check: 4,096 kept + 1: deleted at the second
call; 10,000 kept + 1: at the third; without the resume never in 120 calls.

Switches (read once): EDEN_UP_STALE_TARGETS=1 (exact 1). EDEN_UP_STALE_TARGETS_FRAMES=<n>, digits
only, 3 to 3600, anything else 10 with a warning. On: a start line, and every 10 s
"EDEN_UP_STALE_TARGETS: N stale render targets deleted in the last 10 s (M MiB, T of them sampled
twins); kept K checks as in use, R as read back; S scans resumed; ... deleted so far (... MiB);
I images, texture memory X MiB" (HW_GPU, LOG_INFO). Off: one cached test in TickFrame, one in
JoinImages for render targets and one per present; no flag set, nothing recorded, nothing deleted,
no line.

Left out of the PR: the zero clear of a later image over stale pages (ContentsDefined,
TextureCacheRuntime::ClearImage, vkCmdClearDepthStencilImage) and the PendingTransfer ticks; the
OpenGL flush fix (ef9f08d, OpenGL only); the Catch2 tests. Upstream also skips the deletion when
the stale mark does not cover the image; ours deletes as our collector does (our MarkStale marks
nothing unless EDEN_UP_NO_STALE_UPLOAD or EDEN_UP_SKIP_STALE_UPLOADS is on, and refuses with
frames ahead), so a later image there uploads guest bytes as after any collector delete.

Origin and credit: jpkto, zortos293/Lichii PR #1571 (open), commits 48761a3 ("delete overwritten
render targets after ten unused frames"), 9a3a759 ("count only retirable targets against the retire
cap", from a CodeRabbit review) and fab2816 ("resume the bounded retire scan where it stopped", from
RomainROCH's review); names and paths mapped to our tree (no Lichii rename here).
